      body: 'Here is a made-up story to hold the idea. In a hill town in Japan a girl named Emi dropped her grandmother’s tea bowl, and it broke into four pieces. She hid them in a cloth for a week and could hardly eat. At last she carried the cloth to her grandmother and opened it without a word. The old woman did not scold and did not throw the pieces away. She sat Emi beside her, mixed lacquer with a little gold dust, and joined the pieces slowly, one seam at a time, over many days. When it was done the bowl held tea again, and a line of gold ran where each break had been. Emi asked why she had not hidden the cracks. Her grandmother smiled and said the bowl was not pretending any more, and that it was hers twice now: once when she bought it, and once when she mended it. Emi had tried to hold the pieces together in a cloth. It took other hands to make it one. "He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds." (Psalms 147:3)',
