      body: 'Here is a second made-up story. A farmer named Kofi worked a field beside a river in Ghana. One year the river rose in the night and took the whole crop, the fence, and the small house where his tools were kept. For a long time he would not walk that way. When neighbors spoke of the flood he changed the subject, and he told his children the field had always been poor ground. An old man of the village, Mensah, came and stood with him at the edge of it one evening and did not say much. He only asked Kofi to tell him what had been lost, all of it, and he listened until dark. The next season Mensah came with seed and worked beside him. The flood had left a layer of dark silt, and the yam that year was the heaviest the field had ever given. Kofi did not tell his children the flood had not happened. He told them where the water had reached, and he showed them the harvest. "And I will restore to you the years that the locust hath eaten, the cankerworm, and the caterpiller, and the palmerworm, my great army which I sent among you." (Joel 2:25)',
